#0

Ship basic
Ship basic - Last Edited: 2010-10-05 15:18:52 by kitsero
========================================================
Ship Basic
=========================================
A dock is much like any other shop, you use "buy" and "list" to see what
ships and ship accessories are available at the docks.  You can only buy
a ship at one of the major docks in the world.  An example is Tharnadia on
the Goodie Continent.  To buy your first ship, you must also give it a name.
Names of ship use ANSI commands so you should first use "Testcolor" command
to perfect the name of your ship, otherwise you could end up with a lousy
looking shipname.
The syntax for buying your first ship is "buy hull # <shipname>" where
# is the number of the hull you wish to purchase, and shipname is the
above mentioned name you create using ANSI commands. Use "list hull" to
determine, which hull you want to build and can afford. The smallest and
cheapest ship is sloop. It can not carry weapons but it can transport
a single person from one continent to another.
Ok, now you"ve purchased your ship, the "list" menu at all docks will
have changed.  You can now see 4 various lists.
From now on, you will not have to state a shipname when you change your hull.
Next suggested reading: "help SHIP_LIST"

#0

Ship cargo
Ship cargo - Last Edited: 2013-04-24 18:11:34 by targ
=====================================================
Ship Cargo
=========================================
Sailing cargo is the best way to make money on Duris, but it requires
significant investments. First, you need to buy a cargo ship. Amount
of cargo your ship can carry is shown on hulls list. Bigger ships can
carry a lot of cargo, but they are slow, especially when fully loaded.
The good way to start cargoing is to use smaller cargo ships, they dont
carry much, but move faster and you dont risk as much if something goes
_wrong_.
Each cargo crate weight 2 units, thus fully loaded ships will go MUCH
slower than its max speed. This makes you vulnerable to pirates and other
players attack. Of course, you can jettison you cargo and make a run for it,
but it doesnt always work and cargo worth money. So its good idea to equip
your ship with at least some weapons to be able to fight back.
To buy cargo, go to one of the major docks in the world, and dock your
ship there. Then type "list cargo". This gives you a list of the one type
of cargo you can BUY at this dock, and the many other types of cargo you
can SELL here. It also gives you a list of prices, which are vital to
actually MAKING MONEY, which is the sole purpose of running cargo. Make
sure you buy low and sell high. Stand on the dock you've chosen, with your
ship present and type  "buy cargo #" where # is the number of cargo crates
you wish to buy.
Once you buy the cargo, you sail to a DIFFERENT major dock.  Disembark, and
type "sell cargo". You should type "list cargo" to ensure you"re making a
profit first.  Then, repeat, buy, sail somewhere, sell, get rich...
When someone sells cargo at dock, its price there goes down. When someone
buys cargo, the price goes up. Watch out for other players running cargo
and ruining your profit. If you already bought some cargo, but changed your
plans, you can sell it back to local merchants (at lower price, of course).
The further is your destination dock from the place where certain cargo is
produced, the higher it will sell there. You have to choose between shorter
low-profit trips and longer but profitable ones.
Additionally, you may purchase a diplomat's flag for your ship which will discourage
pirates from attacking you but will incur a 10% tax on any cargo you sold.
Note: Each major docks has it's own type of cargo. Therefore, by number of
types of cargo available, you can determine how many major ports there are,
and even approximate what continent any specific type of cargo can be
purchased at.
Example: Elven Wines are bought on the Elven Continent.
Note: If you summon your ship to another location, all cargo in your ship
will be lost. Use summon only as a last resort if you run cargo.
Note: A sloop and a Yacht have very limited space. You can move some cargo
using these ships, but generally its a waste of time.
Note: If you are attacked during cargo run, and you would rather try to escape
than fight back, or you want to be at full speed to fight back you can
type "order jettison cargo" or "order jettison cargo <n crates>" and your
top speed will go up accordingly.
Note: When cargo is jettisoned (or when ship with cargo is sunk), some of it
stays afloat, and you can pick it up using "order salvage". You can see floating
cargo on map. Even warships, that normally dont carry cargo, can pick some
that way.
Note: Some port merchant sell and buy illegal goods, but they only deal with
well-known captains and experienced crews. If your manage to buy such goods,
hide it well on your ship, because they will be confiscated by authorities
if found. The more crates of cargo you have on your ship, the easier is to
hide crates with contraband.
Next suggested reading: "help SHIP_NPCS"

Divine Boons can increase eligible rewards automatically. Type BOON for
current targets and HELP DIVINE BOONS for rates, expiry and trade limits.

#0

Ship npcs
Ship npcs - Last Edited: 2026-09-27 00:00:00 by Duris
=====================================================
Ship NPCs
=========================================
Pirates and hunters roam the open sea.  While your ship is under way on the
map with no target, there is a small chance at any moment that an NPC ship
appears about 45 squares off your bow and sails straight at you.  Sloops,
yachts and docked ships are never ambushed, and nobody is while the ocean
is at war.
Merchant hulls are ambushed at most once per trip; the count resets when you
dock.  The bigger your hull and the more frags your ship has, the tougher
the pirates: from level 0 raiders in clippers and ketches up to level 3
hunters in destroyers and frigates.  Warships are rarely noticed, but what
finds them is a hunter.
Pirates ram, board and loot.  Their captain carries the key to a treasure
chest aboard, and a pirate ship's frags set the bounty for sinking it.
A diplomat's flag (see "help Ship cargo") makes ambushes much rarer, hunters
pass you over, and a pirate that does attack carries no mind blast cannon.
Cyric's Revenge, a dreadnought, hunts the seas on its own.
Next suggested reading: "help Ship Weapons"

#0

Ship Weapons
Ship Weapons - Last Edited: 2026-09-27 00:00:00 by Duris
=====================================================
Ship Weapons
=========================================
At a shipwright, "list weapons" shows every weapon with its price, weight,
ammunition, range and damage, and the arcs and hulls it fits.  Buy one with
"buy weapon <number> <fore/rear/port/starboard>".
A weapon covers only the arc it is mounted on.  The fore and rear arcs are
80 degrees wide and the port and starboard arcs 100 degrees, so mount
weapons on every side you expect to fight from.
Ballistas fire straight and hit hardest close in.  Catapults lob their shot
over long ranges and scatter it across the target, and their aim suffers
most when the range is changing fast.  Beamcannons do more damage the closer
the target is.  The mind blast cannon does no damage, but stuns the target's
crew: they cannot steer, fire, reload or repair until they recover.
A flying ship is 4 squares up, so it is farther from guns on the water, and
the shortest-reaching weapons cannot hit it at all.  Warships' sails are
built to take fire, and suffer less from every sail hit.
Beamcannons, the mind blast and fragmentation cannons and the Long Tom
catapult are capital weapons.  A ship can carry only one capital item, and
each needs a skilled gun crew or a ship with many frags.
Weapons run out of ammunition; "reload <weapon number/all>" at a dock.  On
the bridge, "look weaponspec" shows your mounts and "look sight <weapon
number>" your chance to hit the locked target.
Next suggested reading: "help Ship Combat"

