Ship crews
Ship crews - Last Edited: 2010-12-16 05:00:37 by Jexni
======================================================
Ship Crews
=========================================
In port towns around the world look around the places sailor gather to have fun or settle deals.
You will find whole crews and principal crew members looking for employment.
Typing 'list' in such places will show you available crews, their specialty and experience.
The better crew is, the more demanding it is to their new captain. There are two ways to meet
their requirements. First is to be at least somewhat famous as a captain (to have a certain
amount of ship frags). Second is to be currently employing a crew, that has higher skill in every
area, than basic skill of the crew you want to hire.
The chief members you can hire are leaders of the crew in one of the aspects of ship handling.
Having these guys aboard helps your crew grow faster in experience and perform their duties better.
They will only work for you if you meet their requirements or your current crew is above certain
skill level in their area.
It is rumored that an certain Golem maker has perfected the construction of
Automaton crews, but most believe this to be just a rumor.
Next suggested reading: "help SHIP_COMMANDS"

#0

Ship list
Ship list - Last Edited: 2014-07-31 23:27:21 by ashyel
======================================================
Ship List
=========================================
This is one of few single commands that will be covered in a helpfile,
simply because this command holds so much information. Using "list" command
at a dock, you can view one of 4 sections:
-Available Hulls
-Weapons
-Equipment
-Cargo
==Hull Upgrades==
This section tells you all the ship classes you can change your
current ship to. A portion of the cost of your current hull will be
deducted from the cost of upgrading to a more expensive hull class.
This means, if you buy a sloop, and then a clipper, it will be more expensive than
simply buying a clipper to start with. However, it will not be as costly
as buying two separate ships (the clipper and the sloop). Again, you will
not need to restate the name of your ship, when you upgrade your hull.
Hulls list looks like this
===================================================================================
                 Load   Cargo  Passenger   Max   Total  Weapons  Epic
    Name         Cap.    Cap.     Cap.    Speed  Armor (F/S/R/P) Cost Cost
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 ) Sloop         5      1         1       100    9     0/0/0/0       100 platinum
2 ) Yacht         12     3         2       100    26    1/1/1/1       300 platinum
3 ) Clipper       55     10        5       88     119   1/2/1/2       1500 platinum
4 ) Ketch         75     20        8       78     165   1/2/1/2       2500 platinum
5 ) Caravel       100    35        10      68     218   1/3/1/3       4000 platinum
6 ) Carrack       135    50        15      58     284   1/3/1/3       8000 platinum
7 ) Galleon       180    70        20      50     363   2/3/1/3       12000 platinum
8 ) Corvette      82     0         6       74     213   1/3/1/3       9000 platinum
9 ) Destroyer     110    0         8       64     285   2/3/1/3       15000 platinum
10) Frigate       142    0         10      55     370   2/3/2/3       22000 platinum
11) Cruiser       200    0         15      48     519   2/4/2/4       36000 platinum
* Load capacity is the max weight this hull can carry. It determines how many weapons/equipment/cargo
you can put on your ship and how it will affects its speed.
* Cargo capacity is the maximum amount of cargo this hull can carry (note: each cargo unit's weight is 2)
Ships below Corvette on the list are pure warships, they can not carry any cargo.
* Passenger capacity determines how many players/mobs can fit on board.
* Maximum speed is the speed the ship will go with minimum load (note: some hulls has certain "free" weight
for weapons and cargo, meaning it will go maximum speed with weapons/cargo of that weight)
* Total armor is the sum of ship's armor on all sides
* Weapon slots determine how many weapons you can install on each side of the ship
==Weapons==
Weapons are the fun part of sailing. Shooting down other players and
their ships! Combat will be covered in "Help SHIP_COMBAT." When you buy a
weapon, you must state what part of the ship the weapon should be mounted
on. You should read "Help SHIP_COMBAT" before you buy weapons, so you can
develop your fighting strategy and buy your weapons to match. To buy a
weapon, you type "buy weapon # <location>" where # is the weapon ID number
when you type "list weapons" at the docks, and location is the part of the
ship you wish to place the weapon.
==================================================================================================
    Name               Weight  Range   Damage  Ammo Disper  Sail  Hull  Sail Reload  Cost
                                                      sion   Hit   Dam   Dam   Time
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1) Small Ballista          3    0-8      2-4    60     10   12%  100%   50%     30  50 platinum
 4) Small Catapult         10   4-15  4 x 2-3    30    160   20%  100%  100%     30  500 platinum
 7) Heavy Ballista         15    0-4    15-22     6     10    0%  100%    0%     30  1000 platinum
* Weight of weapon determine how much this weapon will affect the speed of your ship. Also, every hull has
certain weight restriction for the weapons put on a single side.
* Range determines the maximum reach for this weapon. Some weapons also can not shoot at targets closer
than certain minimum range. Target-to-weapon range ration heavily affects the chance of successfully
hitting the target. Thus, if weapon has range 4, that not only means that you cant shoot at targets
beyond range 4, you will also have hard time hitting something at range 3.
* Damage range shows how powerful the weapon is. Some weapons shoot scattered ammo, their damage shown as
"N x X-Y" - N fragments hitting for X to Y each.
* Ammo is amount of munitions stored on ship for this weapon.
* Dispersion angle show how far the hitting point on target hull can deviate from the direction you are
shooting from. Dispersion 360 means that target can be hit at any side.
* Normally, you target enemy hull with your weapons, but sometimes you hit their sail instead. "Sail hit"
determines the probability of hitting the sail.
* Some weapons due to nature of their ammo dont do full damage to target's hull or sail.
"Hull dam" and "Sail dam" define how good is weapon in each case.
* Reload time defines how long it will take for your crew to get weapon ready to fire again.
Some weapons have additional requirements to buy or can not be installed on the hull you have. Such weapons
will be dimmed on the list.
==Eqiupment==
There is a list of additional equipment you can install at your ship. Each equipment has its own unique effect.
===============================================
    Name                  Weight  Cost
-----------------------------------------------
 1) Bronze Plated Ram         14  330 platinum
 2) Zentharium Levistone       9  5000 platinum
* Equipping your ship with the ram worth it if ramming is part of your combat tactics. It does additional
damage to target and partially protects your fore side during ramming .
* Levistone is a magical monolith that is able to generate a powerful force of levitation, floating your
ship into air for a short period of time.

#0

Ship looks
Ship looks - Last Edited: 2010-10-05 15:24:39 by kitsero
========================================================
Ship Looks
=========================================
==Look Out==
This command looks outside your ship, at the immediate surroundings. If
you are in a dock zone, it will tell you the available exits much like when
you are walking around normally. This will tell you other ships in your
square, and it will show you a small portion of the worldmap (in squares)
when you are outside.
==Look Tactical==
look tactical (optional parameters are <X> <Y>)
This will show you a tactical map centered on you (or x,y with 50,50
being the center of the map -ie YOU). It is displayed as a hex grid and
any nearby ships will be displayed according to their ID. The Hex grid is
the square setup basically "converted" to a hex grid, so it is not always
perfect in appearance. If the optional parameters <X> and <Y> are given the
map will center on that coord if it is within 35 hexes. This is a
calculated value, if you provide numbers outside the range, you will see an
error message. You cannot Look Tactical inside a dock zone.
==Look cargo==
This will tell you what cargo you currently have on your ship, and the
price you paid for that cargo.
==Look Status/Look Ship==
This will show you a detailed status of your ship with the state of your hull, armor and sails,
weapon status (whether its damaged, reloading or ready to fire) and ammo remaining.
It will also show your speed, heading and target. The ID number on
the upper right corner of the status display is your own current ID.
==Look Weaponspec==
This will show you the specifications of all your weapons. It will show
their weapon ranges and damage each shot will do as well as if the weapon
is destroyed or not.
==Look Sight <weapon number>==
If your have a target this command will show you your percentage to hit
the current locked target with that particular weapon.
==Look Contacts==
This will display a list of all ships within a scan range of your ship.
This is the heart of getting info on your target. The list is broken
into several parts and will look kinda like this:
Contact listing
[AN] "NorgDog"         X:53  Y:50  R:3.0   B:90  H:0   S:0 |SP
"AN" is the ship"s ID this is the parameter you pass to lock to lock onto
this target.
"Norgdog" is the ship"s name.
X:53 and Y:50 are their coords on your tactical map, so if you do a
"look tactical 53 50", it will center the map on Norgdog.
R:3.0 is Range, so in this case, Norgdog is 3 Squares from you.
B:90 is their bearing to you, so in this case, Norgdog is to your east
since 90 is east, so if you were to head east, you would be facing
Norgdog.
H:0 is Norgdog"s heading.. so in this case, Norgdog is facing north
since 0 is North.
S:0 is Norgdog"s speed so in this case, its not moving since it"s 0.
The first letter after separator (S), indicates it is on your starboard firing arc, so if you have
any weapons on your starboard side, they can fire at Norgdog.
The second letter (P) indicates, that Norgdog faces you with its port side, thus
you are likely to hit that side with your weapons (if their dispersion angle
is small).
There can be a third letter at the end of contact line:
blue   'F' - ship is floating in air
yellow 'A' - ship is anchored
red    'S' - ship is sinking
Note: White 'H:' and 'S:' on top of contact list show your own current heading and speed.
Note: when sailing, you should check your contacts as often as possible. This way you notice
any incoming danger early and can react in time.
Next suggested reading: "help SHIP_CARGO"

#0

Ship movement
Ship movement - Last Edited: 2010-10-05 15:22:08 by kitsero
===========================================================
Ship Movement
=========================================
Once you have purchased your ship and sail, you are ready to sail the seas,
pillage ships, run cargo, and travel the world.
==Dock Movement==
When you are docked, you cannot unfurl your sails and take off safely.
You must first maneuver out to the open seas, away from the docks. Some
docks have complex zones, much like a normal zone, except it"s all water.
You still maneuver in "rooms" dictating N,S,E, or W as the direction.
The command to maneuver is: "Order Maneuver <direction>"
==World Movement==
The ship system works on a principle of speed and heading. What this means
is, if you set your ship to a certain speed, it will continue moving until
the speed is set to 0. Heading is the direction that the ship is pointing
and hence will be the direction the ship will be traveling. Heading is
done from a 0-360 degree circle with north being 0, east being 90, south
being 180, and west being 270.
Also affecting the ships is acceleration and turn rate. When a speed is
set, the ship does not immediately jump to that speed, it has to accelerate
to the set speed. Acceleration is faster on smaller ships. On the same note,
when a heading is set, the ship does not immediately face that direction,
it has to turn to indicated direction, this is the turn rate. Turn rate is
also higher on smaller ships than large Dreadnaughts, so theoretically a
small fast Clipper can run circles around a Galleon. Turn rate is also affected
by the speed of the ship, the faster a ship is traveling the faster they turn.
Immobile ship can be still turned by paddling, but turn rate is extremely slow.
==Docking==
In order to re-dock your ship, you must be adjacent to the dock zone, or
adjacent to an area of land which is dockable, and you again use
the "order maneuver <direction>" command as you use when you are leaving
the docks. Not all shores and coasts are dockable You are expected to find
these other locations yourself, however if another ship is already docked there,
this is a good indicator. To test a spot, simply sail alongside the square,
slow to speed 20 or less, and attempt to maneuver onto the shore.
Beware: if you attempt to SAIL into a shore, you have a chance
of CRASHING, even if the zone is a dock. Your ship crew will try to prevent this,
but they cannot be relied upon every time.  You must also be traveling at
speed 20 or less in order to attempt to maneuver.
Note: You CAN NOT re-dock (or summon) your ship, if it is in the middle of
combat!
Next suggested reading: "help Ship Combat"

